course: jobava
name: Jobava London
color: white
eco: D00 A45 D01 A40
tagline: The London with its gloves off. Nc3, Bf4, Nb5, and castle long.
description: The Jobava London, or Rapport-Jobava System, is the London's aggressive cousin. Instead of the quiet c3 and Nbd2, White plays Nc3 and Bf4 and aims straight at Black's weak points. The knight jumps to b5 to hit c7 with the f4 bishop, the queen goes to d2 or d3, White often castles queenside and throws the h-pawn up the board, and the e4 break or f3 with g4 punishes an early ...Bf5. This course teaches the main 3...e6 lines with Nb5 and Black's answers ...Bd6, ...Bb4, ...a6 and ...Na6, the sharp 3...c5 with ...Qb6 and ...Qa5, the ...Bf5 and ...c6 setups, the King's Indian style ...g6, the tricky 3...Nc6, and every 1...Nf6 move order. Along the way you learn the traps that make this system so dangerous at club level: Nc7 forks, h-file sacrifices, and the one move order mistake you must never make.

chapter: nb5
title: The main line: 3...e6 and the Nb5 jump
intro: After 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3, Black's most natural moves all meet the same idea: Nb5. The knight and the f4 bishop both hit c7, so Black has to spend time defending it, and if Black plays ...Bd6 the knight can take the bishop. Black's best answers are to trade on f4, to check on b4, to prevent Nb5 with ...a6, or to cover c7 with ...Na6. Learn which version of Nb5 fits each one, and when to switch plans to h4 or the e4 break instead. Note that 3...e6 4.e3 c5 is the same position as 3...c5 4.e3 e6, covered in the next chapter.

line: e6-bd6-bxf4
name: 4...Bd6 5.Nb5 Bxf4: the doubled f-pawns
moves: 1.d4 {Claim the centre.} d5 2.Nc3 {The Jobava move. The knight blocks the c-pawn, but it gets to b5 fast and supports the e4 break.} Nf6 3.Bf4 {Now the bishop and knight work together against c7. This is the starting position of the Jobava London.} e6 4.e3 {Open the f1 bishop and support d4. Now Nb5 is a real threat, because c7 is attacked twice.} Bd6 {Black copies the London idea and offers to trade bishops.} 5.Nb5 {Jump on the d6 bishop. If Black ignores it, Nxd6 gives you the bishop pair.} Bxf4 {The most solid answer: trade before the knight can take on d6.} 6.exf4 {Recapture away from the centre on purpose. The f4 pawn controls e5, and the e-file opens for your rook.} c6 7.Nc3 {The knight has done its job by forcing the trade, so it returns to the centre.} O-O 8.Nf3 b6 9.Ne5 {The doubled f-pawns make e5 a rock-solid outpost: no black pawn can ever kick the knight away.} Bb7 10.h4 {Typical Jobava aggression. The h-pawn marches, and you can castle either side later.} c5 11.h5 {Gain more space and prepare h6 to weaken the long diagonal.} h6 12.g4 {Open lines against Black's king. Your plan is Qf3, Bd3, castle long and push g5. Black will counter with ...cxd4 and pressure on the centre, so keep the knight on e5 strong.}

line: e6-bd6-bb4
name: 4...Bd6 5.Nb5 Bb4+: trapping the bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3 Bd6 5.Nb5 Bb4+ {Black dodges the knight with check.} 6.c3 {Block and gain time: the bishop must move again.} Ba5 {Black keeps the bishop on the diagonal.} 7.a4 {The bishop is short of squares. The threat is b4, trapping it on a5.} a6 {Black attacks the knight, hoping you'll retreat.} 8.b4 {Counterattack: if Black takes the knight, you take the bishop.} Nc6 9.bxa5 {Take the bishop first.} axb5 10.Bxb5 {Now you win back the pawn, and the c6 knight is pinned.} Rxa5 11.Nf3 {You have the better structure and the bishop pair in an open position.} Bd7 12.O-O O-O 13.Bd3 {Keep the bishops. Plans are Qc2, Rb1 hitting b7, and the e4 break. Black's rook on a5 is clumsy.}

line: e6-bd6-nc6
name: 4...Bd6 5.Nb5 Nc6: take the bishop
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3 Bd6 5.Nb5 Nc6 {Black lets the knight take on d6.} 6.Nxd6+ {Take it. The bishop pair is a lasting edge, and Black's pawn on d6 blocks his own pieces.} cxd6 7.Nf3 Ne4 8.Bd3 {Develop and challenge the e4 knight.} Qe7 9.h3 {Make room for the bishop on h2, so ...e5 doesn't come with tempo.} e5 10.Bh2 {The bishop stays on the long diagonal, pressing on e5 and d6. Next is O-O and c4 to open the position for your two bishops.}

line: e6-be7-na6
name: 4...Be7 5.Nb5 Na6: covering c7
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3 Be7 {The Queen's Gambit setup, keeping the bishop away from your knight.} 5.Nb5 {Hit c7 anyway.} Na6 {The standard defence: the knight guards c7. But on a6 it is far from the kingside.} 6.Nf3 O-O 7.h4 {With Black's knight on the rim, there are fewer defenders near his king. Start the h-pawn.} c6 {Kick the knight.} 8.Nc3 {Back to c3, with your job done: Black's knight is offside.} Qb6 {Hit b2.} 9.Rb1 {Simply protect the pawn.} c5 10.a3 {Take b4 away from Black's pieces.} Bd7 11.Ne5 {The outpost. It eyes d7 and f7.} Be8 12.g4 {A full kingside pawn storm. Next g5 drives away the f6 knight, then h5 and Bd3 aim at h7.}

line: e6-bb4
name: 4...Bb4: the Nimzo-style pin
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3 Bb4 {Black pins the knight, so Nb5 is impossible for now.} 5.a3 {Ask the question at once. If the bishop retreats to d6, Nb5 is back on.} Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {You get the bishop pair, and the c3 pawn supports d4.} O-O 7.Nf3 c5 8.c4 {Open the position for your bishops. Your doubled c-pawn is no longer a weakness once it moves.} cxd4 9.exd4 Nc6 10.Bd3 {Aim at h7.} dxc4 11.Bxc4 b6 12.O-O Bb7 13.Re1 {An isolated d-pawn with two bishops. Your plan is d5 at the right moment, or Ne5 with an attack on the king.}

line: e6-a6
name: 4...a6: stopping Nb5
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 e6 4.e3 a6 {Black takes b5 away from your knight.} 5.Bd3 {Develop and aim at h7. Without Nb5, your plan becomes the e4 break.} c5 6.dxc5 {Take, and gain a tempo on the bishop when it recaptures.} Bxc5 7.Nf3 Nc6 8.O-O O-O 9.e4 {The central break. Your knight on c3 is perfectly placed to support it.} dxe4 10.Nxe4 Nxe4 11.Bxe4 {Your bishops point at the king and you're ahead in development. Next Qe2 and Rad1, with Ng5 ideas against h7.}

chapter: c5
title: 3...c5: the sharpest reply
intro: 3...c5 hits d4 at once, and here the move order matters most. Never play 4.Nb5 before e3: Black answers ...Qa5 check and wins the knight, because nothing guards b5. After 4.e3, your f1 bishop covers b5, and Nb5 becomes a monster. Black's main tries are ...cxd4 with ...a6, ...e6 with ...Na6, and the queen sorties ...Qb6 and ...Qa5. The natural ...Nc6 is a trap that loses material to Nb5.

line: c5-cxd4
name: 4...cxd4 5.exd4 a6: the open c-file
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 {The only good move. 4.Nb5? runs into ...Qa5 check, winning the knight. After e3, the f1 bishop guards b5.} cxd4 5.exd4 {Recapture with the pawn. The e-file opens and the f4 bishop stays outside the pawn chain.} a6 {Black stops Nb5 for good.} 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.Ne5 {Grab the outpost before Black can play ...Bg4.} e6 8.Nxc6 {Trade, and Black's pawns are left on c6 and d5, blocking his own bishop.} bxc6 9.Na4 {The knight heads for c5, the hole Black's queenside left behind.} Bd6 10.Bxd6 Qxd6 11.Be2 O-O 12.O-O {A small but lasting edge: Nc5, c3 and Qd2 keep Black's queenside under pressure.}

line: c5-bg4
name: 4...cxd4 5.exd4 Bg4: hitting the queen
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 cxd4 5.exd4 Bg4 {The bishop attacks your queen through e2.} 6.f3 {Kick the bishop. Your knight is not on f3 yet, so f3 costs you nothing.} Bd7 7.Nb5 {Now c7 is the target.} Bxb5 8.Bxb5+ {You have the bishop pair, and Black has lost his good bishop.} Nc6 9.c3 e6 10.Ne2 {The knight goes to e2, since your pawn is on f3. Next O-O and Qd3, with the bishops pointing at both wings.}

line: c5-e6-nb5
name: 4...e6 5.Nb5 Na6: the main line
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 e6 {Black builds a French-style centre.} 5.Nb5 {Now Nb5 is safe: ...Qa5 check is met by c3, and the bishop on f1 guards the knight. The threat is Nc7 check.} Na6 {The best defence. 5...Qa5 check 6.c3 Na6 7.a4 also keeps the knight on b5.} 6.c3 {Support the knight and d4, so ...c4 and ...Qb6 don't hurt.} Be7 7.Nf3 O-O 8.h4 {The rook joins along the h-file. Avoid 8.Bd3?: ...c4 hits the bishop and opens the way for ...Qb6 against b5 and b2.} Bd7 9.a4 {Anchor the knight on b5.} c4 10.b3 {Strike at the c4 pawn at once.} cxb3 11.Qxb3 {Your queen eyes b7 and d5. Plans are Bd3, Ne5 and the h-pawn push. Black's knight on a6 is still out of play.}

line: c5-nc6-trap
name: 4...Nc6? 5.Nb5: the c7 fork
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 Nc6 {Natural, but a serious mistake: the knight can no longer go to a6 to guard c7.} 5.Nb5 {Threatens Nc7 check, forking king and rook.} e6 {A natural move that loses at once. Black's only try is 5...e5, a pawn sacrifice.} 6.Nc7+ {Fork! The queen can't take, because Bxc7 would win her.} Kd7 7.Nxa8 {You've won a whole rook. Get the knight out with c3 and Nb6 if it is attacked, or trade it for a piece.}

line: c5-nc6-e5
name: 4...Nc6? 5.Nb5 e5: the pawn sacrifice
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 Nc6 5.Nb5 e5 {Black gives a pawn to get his pieces out.} 6.dxe5 {Take it, and the f6 knight is attacked too.} Qa5+ {Black needs the check to deal with Nc7.} 7.Qd2 {Offer the queen trade. Black has nothing better than to take.} Qxd2+ 8.Kxd2 {In the endgame your king is safe, and Nc7 is still a threat.} Ne4+ 9.Ke1 g5 {Black tries to win back material by hitting the f4 bishop.} 10.f3 {Kick the knight first. You stay a pawn up with a better position, and Nc7 check is still in the air.}

line: c5-qb6
name: 4...Qb6: hitting b2
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 Qb6 {Black attacks b2, and ...Qxb2 would hit c3.} 5.Nb5 {Counterattack. The knight hits c7, and Black has no time to grab b2 because Nc7 check comes first.} Na6 6.dxc5 {Hit the queen.} Qxc5 7.Nf3 e6 8.a4 {Secure the knight on b5. If Black plays ...a6 later, the knight can drop back to d4, a great central square.} Be7 9.c3 O-O 10.Be2 {Solid development. Next O-O and Nbd4 or Qb3, and Black's a6 knight still struggles to find a role.}

line: c5-qa5
name: 4...Qa5: the pinning queen
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c5 4.e3 Qa5 {Black pins the knight to stop Nb5 and e4.} 5.dxc5 {Grab the pawn. Black has to spend time winning it back.} Nc6 6.Bb5 {Pin the knight with a pin of your own. Quiet moves like 6.Nf3 let Black win back the pawn with an easy game.} a6 7.Bxc6+ {Give up the bishop to wreck Black's pawns.} bxc6 8.Nf3 Qxc5 9.O-O {Black has the bishop pair, but his pawns are weak. Next Na4, c3 and Qd2, and c5 is a lovely square for your knight.}

chapter: bf5
title: ...Bf5 and the Slav setups
intro: When Black develops the light-squared bishop to f5, the Jobava London has a sharp answer: f3, g4 and h4 hunt the bishop, then e4 hits the centre. Your king goes to the queenside and the pawns become an attack. Against 3...c6, the same plan works, with Bd3 to trade off the bishop. And when Black plays 3...a6 to stop Nb5, Bd3 and h4-h5 make the f5 bishop pay.

line: bf5-e6
name: 3...Bf5 4.f3: the g4 hunt
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 Bf5 {Black copies your bishop.} 4.f3 {Prepare g4 and e4. The quiet 4.e3 is also fine, but f3 is the Jobava way.} e6 5.g4 {Hit the bishop.} Bg6 6.h4 {Now h5 threatens to trap the bishop.} h5 {Black stops your h-pawn, but leaves g5 for your pawn.} 7.g5 {Kick the f6 knight.} Nfd7 8.e4 {The central break. Your pawns control the kingside, and the e4 pawn hits d5.} Nb6 9.Qd2 {Get ready to castle long.} Nc6 10.O-O-O Bb4 11.Bd3 {Defend e4 and finish development. Your king is safe on the queenside, and Nge2, Kb1 and a central push with d5 or e5 come next.}

line: bf5-c5
name: 3...Bf5 4.f3 c5: the e4 gambit
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 Bf5 4.f3 c5 {Black hits d4 at once.} 5.e4 {The critical move. Quiet moves like e3 or dxc5 lose time, and Nb5? runs into ...Qa5 check.} dxe4 6.d5 {Gain space instead of taking back. Black's pieces get cramped.} Nbd7 7.Qe2 {Attack the e4 pawn and prepare to castle long.} e5 8.dxe6 Bxe6 9.O-O-O {Your rook lands on the open d-file opposite Black's queen. You will win the e4 pawn back with Nxe4 or fxe4, and Black's king is still in the centre.}

line: c6-bf5
name: 3...c6 and ...Bf5: the Slav setup
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c6 {Black takes b5 away from your knight.} 4.e3 {Solid development first.} Bf5 5.f3 {The same idea as against 3...Bf5: prepare g4.} e6 6.g4 Bg6 7.h4 h5 8.g5 Nfd7 9.Bd3 {Trade off Black's best bishop.} Bxd3 10.Qxd3 g6 11.e4 {Now the e4 break. You have more space everywhere, and your queen is ready for O-O-O.}

line: c6-bg4
name: 3...c6 4.e3 Bg4: the bishop on g4
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 c6 4.e3 Bg4 {Black develops the bishop before ...e6, eyeing your queen on d1.} 5.f3 {Kick the bishop.} Bh5 6.g4 {Again! The bishop is chased.} Bg6 7.h4 {Threaten h5, trapping the bishop.} h6 8.h5 Bh7 9.Bd3 {Trade off the bishop that the pawns have shut out.} Bxd3 10.Qxd3 e6 11.O-O-O {Your king is safe, and the kingside pawns give you a big space advantage. Nge2, Kb1 and e4 come next.}

line: a6-bf5
name: 3...a6 and ...Bf5
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 a6 {Black prevents Nb5 before anything else.} 4.e3 {Develop and keep options open.} Bf5 5.Bd3 {Offer the bishop trade. Here f3 is playable too, but Bd3 makes ...a6 look slow.} Bg6 {Black refuses to trade.} 6.h4 {Then h5 will trap the bishop or force ...Bxd3.} e6 7.h5 Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {Your queen is active and ready for O-O-O.} h6 9.O-O-O {Opposite sides are coming. Your h-pawn is already halfway to Black's king, with g4-g5 next.}

line: a6-e6
name: 3...a6 4.e3 e6: transposition
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 a6 4.e3 e6 5.Bd3 {The same position as 3...e6 4.e3 a6. Plan the e4 break.}

chapter: fianchetto
title: 3...g6 and 3...Nc6
intro: Against the fianchetto with 3...g6, the Jobava has a direct plan: h4 and h5, and if Black castles early the h-file opens and Bh6 takes off his best defender. Against 3...Nc6, Nb5 is stronger than ever, because the c6 knight no longer guards c7. Black must answer with the ...e5 pawn sacrifice, and every quiet move loses to Nxc7.

line: g6-castles
name: 3...g6 4...O-O: the h-file attack
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 g6 {A Grünfeld-style setup.} 4.e3 Bg7 5.h4 {The Jobava answer to the fianchetto: the h-pawn heads straight for g6.} O-O {Castling into the attack is already a small inaccuracy.} 6.h5 {Open the h-file.} c5 7.hxg6 hxg6 8.Qd2 {The queen and bishop line up on h6.} cxd4 9.exd4 Nc6 10.Bh6 {Trade off the g7 bishop, the key defender. Then Qh6 or Qg5 and Rh-rook lifts give a crushing attack down the open h-file.}

line: g6-nxh5
name: 6...Nxh5?: the rook sacrifice
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 g6 4.e3 Bg7 5.h4 O-O 6.h5 Nxh5 {Black grabs the pawn, but opens the h-file himself.} 7.Rxh5 {A classic sacrifice: give the rook for the knight to rip open the king.} gxh5 8.Qxh5 {Your queen arrives on h5 and Black's kingside is wrecked. Follow up with Bd3, O-O-O and Rh1, or Bh6 to strip the king.}

line: g6-c6
name: 3...g6 5...c6: the calm approach
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 g6 4.e3 Bg7 5.h4 c6 {Black keeps the king in the centre for now and prepares ...Qb6.} 6.Nf3 {Don't rush h5 here: 6.h5? Nxh5 and with the king still on e8 there's no sacrifice. Develop first.} Nbd7 7.Be2 O-O 8.Ne5 {Now the knight takes the outpost, and your h-pawn is still ready to go. Plans are Qd2, O-O-O and h5.}

line: nc6-e5
name: 3...Nc6 4.Nb5 e5: the pawn sacrifice
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 Nc6 {The Chigorin-style knight. It blocks the c-pawn, so c7 is weak.} 4.Nb5 {Hit c7 straight away. It is attacked twice and defended only by the queen.} e5 {Black's only good answer: a pawn sacrifice that opens lines.} 5.dxe5 {Take it, hitting the f6 knight.} a6 6.Nc3 {Back to c3, where the knight pressures d5.} Nh5 7.Nxd5 {Grab a second pawn.} Be6 8.e4 Nxf4 9.Nxf4 Qxd1+ 10.Rxd1 {You're two pawns up for now. Black can win one back with ...Bxa2, but you stay a clean pawn up in an endgame, with a strong extra e-pawn.}

line: nc6-trap
name: 3...Nc6 4.Nb5 Bf5?: the c7 fork
moves: 1.d4 d5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Bf4 Nc6 4.Nb5 Bf5 {A natural developing move, and a blunder.} 5.Nxc7+ {Fork! If ...Qxc7, Bxc7 wins the queen.} Kd7 6.Nxa8 {You've won a rook. Most quiet moves here, like ...e6 or ...Bg4, lose to the same fork.}

chapter: nf6
title: 1...Nf6 move orders
intro: Most 1...Nf6 players will transpose with 2...d5. Against the others: 2...g6 gets the Qd2, O-O-O and Bh6 attack; 2...e6 is met by 3.Bf4, keeping the system; and 2...c5 is answered with 3.d5, taking space.

line: nf6-d5
name: 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 d5: back to the main line
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 {The Jobava move order works against 1...Nf6 too.} d5 3.Bf4 {The main position. Everything from the earlier chapters applies.}

line: nf6-g6
name: 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 g6: the King's Indian
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 g6 3.Bf4 {The Jobava answer to the King's Indian. Your bishop is ready to go to h6.} Bg7 4.Qd2 {Queen and bishop line up for Bh6.} d6 5.e4 {Take the whole centre.} O-O 6.O-O-O {Castle long and get ready to attack.} c6 7.Bh6 {Trade the g7 bishop, Black's best defender.} b5 8.f3 {Secure e4 so your knights don't get kicked around.} b4 9.Nce2 c5 10.h4 {Your h-pawn is the battering ram.} Qa5 11.Kb1 {Step out of any checks. Always put the king on b1 when Black's queen comes to the queenside.} Nc6 12.Bxg7 Kxg7 13.h5 {Open the h-file. A classic race, but your attack arrives first.}

line: nf6-e6-d5
name: 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 e6 3.Bf4 d5: transposition
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 e6 3.Bf4 {Stay in the system.} d5 4.e3 {The main 3...e6 position. Now Nb5 ideas begin.}

line: nf6-e6-bb4
name: 2...e6 3.Bf4 Bb4: the Nimzo-style pin
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 e6 3.Bf4 Bb4 {Black pins the knight before ...d5.} 4.Qd3 {Unpin by protecting c3, and prepare e4 and O-O-O.} d5 5.a3 {Ask the bishop.} Bd6 6.Bxd6 {Here you can trade: Black has to take back with the c-pawn.} cxd6 7.e4 {The e4 break, and Black's d6 pawn is a target.} dxe4 8.Nxe4 Nxe4 9.Qxe4 {A central queen and a better structure. Next Nf3, Bd3 and O-O-O.}

line: nf6-e6-c5
name: 2...e6 3.Bf4 c5: take space with d5
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 e6 3.Bf4 c5 {Black hits d4.} 4.d5 {Push past. The knight on c3 supports d5 perfectly.} exd5 5.Nxd5 Nxd5 6.Qxd5 Nc6 7.O-O-O {Your queen is centralised and the d-file is yours. Black's d-pawn is backward, and the f4 bishop stops ...d6 from being easy.} d6 8.e4 {Grab space and support the queen.} Be6 9.Qd2 {Plans are Nf3, Bb5 and pressure against d6. Your king is safe on c1.}

line: nf6-c5
name: 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 c5: the Benoni
moves: 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3 c5 3.d5 {Take space: this is the best answer.} d6 4.e4 g6 5.Nf3 Bg7 6.Be2 O-O 7.O-O e6 8.Bf4 {There's the Jobava bishop, hitting d6.} exd5 9.exd5 {A Benoni structure where your pieces are better placed. Plans are Re1, Nd2 and Nc4 to target d6.}

quiz: c5-cxd4 6
q: Black has just played 3...c5. Why is 4.Nb5 a mistake right now?
a: ...Qa5 check wins the knight, so play 4.e3 first
w: Black can trap it with ...a6
w: It leaves d4 undefended
why: After 4.Nb5? Qa5 check, the knight on b5 is attacked and nothing guards it. Once you've played e3, the f1 bishop covers b5, and Nb5 becomes a real threat.

quiz: nc6-trap 8
q: Black has played ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 against your knight on b5. What now?
a: Nxc7: fork the king and rook
w: e3: develop calmly
w: Nxa7: grab a pawn
why: The c6 knight no longer guards c7, so it is attacked by the knight and the f4 bishop but defended only by the queen. If the queen takes, Bxc7 wins her.

quiz: g6-castles 18
q: Black has castled into your h-file attack. What's the key move?
a: Bh6: trade off the g7 bishop
w: Nf3: develop first
w: O-O-O: castle long first
why: The g7 bishop is the most important defender of Black's king. Once it is gone, Qh6 and the open h-file make the attack very hard to stop.

quiz: bf5-c5 8
q: After 3...Bf5 4.f3, Black hits your centre with 4...c5. What's the critical answer?
a: e4: strike in the centre, gambit style
w: e3: solid development
w: Nb5: hit c7
why: With f3 already played, you have to strike quickly. e4 opens lines while Black's king is still in the centre. Nb5 fails to ...Qa5 check.
